# Cloudflare Pages equivalent of netlify.toml's [[redirects]] rules — see
# that file's comments for the reasoning. Kept in sync by hand during the
# Netlify -> Cloudflare Pages migration; once Netlify is fully retired,
# netlify.toml can go and this becomes the only copy.
#
# No "force" flag here (unlike netlify.toml's force = true): confirmed
# against Cloudflare's docs that Pages redirects always apply, even when
# the request path matches a real file in the deployment — Netlify's
# default is the opposite (a non-forced redirect is skipped if a real file
# matches), which is why netlify.toml needed force = true on the rules
# below to begin with. Order matters here exactly like it does there:
# first match wins, so the catch-all stays last.

/find-talent.html  /for-employers.html#categories  301

# "publish = '.'" on Netlify (and the equivalent root-as-output-directory
# setup on Cloudflare Pages) means this repo's ENTIRE git-tracked tree is
# otherwise servable as static files, not just the marketing pages --
# backend/ (Express source), platform/ (the Next.js app's own source,
# deployed separately), docs/ (internal stage write-ups, including
# known-gap notes) and .github/ would otherwise be directly fetchable.
/backend/*   /404.html  404
/platform/*  /404.html  404
/docs/*      /404.html  404
/.github/*   /404.html  404
/.git/*      /404.html  404
/render.yaml /404.html  404

/*  /404.html  404
